The given light wave is a superposition of two waves with angular frequencies:
\omega_1 = 3 \times 10^{15} \text{ rad/sec}
\omega_2 = 12 \times 10^{15} \text{ rad/sec}
For the photoelectric effect, the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectron depends on the highest frequency component of the incident light. Therefore, we consider \(\omega_{max} = \omega_2\).
\omega_{max} = 12 \times 10^{15} \text{ rad/sec}
The frequency \(\nu\) is related to angular frequency \(\omega\) by:
\nu = \frac{\omega}{2\pi}
So, the frequency of the incident photon is:
\nu = \frac{12 \times 10^{15}}{2\pi} = \frac{6}{\pi} \times 10^{15} \approx 1.91 \times 10^{15} \text{ Hz}
The energy of the incident photon is:
E_{photon} = h\nu = (6.6 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J-s}) \times (1.91 \times 10^{15} \text{ Hz})
E_{photon} = 1.2606 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J}
Convert the photon energy from Joules to electron Volts (eV):
E_{photon} = \frac{1.2606 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C/eV}} \approx 7.878 \text{ eV}
Rounding to one decimal place, \(E_{photon} \approx 7.9 \text{ eV}\).
The maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectron \(K_{max}\) is given by Einstein's photoelectric equation:
K_{max} = E_{photon} - \phi_0
Given work function \(\phi_0 = 2.8 \text{ eV}\).
K_{max} = 7.9 \text{ eV} - 2.8 \text{ eV}
K_{max} = 5.1 \text{ eV}
Thus, the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectron is approximately \(5.1\) eV.

